INT. MERIDIAN INSURANCE - BASEMENT FILE ROOM - MORNING

Gray shelves. Gray boxes. One flickering fluorescent tube. A sign: "REMEMBER: DOCUMENTATION IS PROTECTION."

GERALD PLUMMER (50s, short-sleeved dress shirt, lanyard) wheels a cart of folders down the aisle, whistling a tune with no melody. He stops at a shelf, squints at a label.

A woman steps out of the shadows. She wears an expensive coat. No lanyard. No badge. A STRANGER.

STRANGER
Excuse me. Are you the file clerk?

GERALD
Archivist.

STRANGER
Archivist. I'm looking for the Henderson claim.

GERALD
Which Henderson?

STRANGER
The big one.

GERALD
They're all big, miss.

She smiles thinly and holds out a hand.

STRANGER
I'm Dana. Dana Whitfield. From corporate.

GERALD
(delighted)
Oh! The audit.

DANA
Not an audit. A review.

GERALD
Gerald Plummer. Twenty-six years in this basement.

DANA
Impressive.

GERALD
It's a very nice basement.

A third figure appears at the end of the aisle. HARRIET OKAFOR-BLYTH (30s, headset, tablet) walks briskly toward them, typing as she goes.

HARRIET
Gerald, I was told you'd be upstairs for the town hall.

GERALD
I was told the town hall was optional.

HARRIET
It's mandatory.

GERALD
Then who told me it was optional?

HARRIET
Dana, were you speaking to him?

DANA
Briefly.

HARRIET
Did you mention the restructuring?

DANA
I did not.

GERALD
What restructuring?

Harriet and Dana exchange a look. The light flickers.

HARRIET
(carefully)
Gerald, why don't you come upstairs.

GERALD
Why?

DANA
There's cake.

GERALD
Is it a retirement cake?

DANA
It could be.

Gerald looks down at the cart. At the Henderson folder on top.

GERALD
Then I'd better file this first.

He wheels the cart away, whistling, as Dana and Harriet watch him go.
